>
  <section aria-labelledby="three-labels">
    <h2 id="three-labels">Separate three kinds of product labels</h2>
    <p>
      A label such as “¾-inch gravel,” a grading such as #57, and a regional
      name such as crusher run answer different questions. The first suggests a
      size, the second points to a grading system, and the third may describe a
      locally sold blend. None alone establishes the material’s mineral type,
      washing, bulk weight or correct use on your site.
    </p>
    <div class="table-responsive">
      <table>
        <caption>
          Questions to ask about the words on an aggregate quote
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Label type</th>
            <th scope="col">Example</th>
            <th scope="col">Information still needed</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Nominal product size</th>
            <td>¾-inch stone</td>
            <td>
              Actual grading, oversize and smaller fractions, fines and wash
              status
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Numbered size designation</th>
            <td>#57 or #67</td>
            <td>
              Governing specification, sieve results, source and intended layer
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Minus product</th>
            <td>¾-inch minus</td>
            <td>
              The complete particle-size blend and whether its fines meet the
              project requirements
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Regional or supplier name</th>
            <td>QP, DGA, 21-A or CA-6</td>
            <td>
              Exact product designation; names from different agencies are not
              automatically interchangeable
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="sieves">
    <h2 id="sieves">ASTM D448 / AASHTO M43 coarse aggregate sieves</h2>
    <p>
      Sieve analysis separates a sample through a series of square openings and
      reports the percentage passing each sieve. A larger opening passes more of
      the sample, while numbered sieves (such as No. 4, No. 8, or No. 200) use
      designated wire-cloth openings rather than inch fractions.
    </p>
    <div class="table-responsive">
      <table>
        <caption>
          Reference coarse aggregate size designations per ASTM D448 / AASHTO
          M43
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Size #</th>
            <th scope="col">Nominal Size</th>
            <th scope="col">Key Sieve Limits (% Passing)</th>
            <th scope="col">Typical Project Uses</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">#1</th>
            <td>3½ to 1½ in</td>
            <td>90–100% passing 3½ in; 0–15% passing 1½ in</td>
            <td>
              Heavy subbase stabilization, gabions, construction entrances
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">#4</th>
            <td>1½ to ¾ in</td>
            <td>90–100% passing 1½ in; 0–15% passing ¾ in</td>
            <td>
              Structural drainage courses, heavy backfill, primary reservoir
              layers
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">#57</th>
            <td>1 in to No. 4</td>
            <td>
              95–100% passing 1 in; 25–60% passing ½ in; 0–10% passing No. 4
            </td>
            <td>
              French drains, pipe bedding, concrete aggregate, clean driveways
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">#67</th>
            <td>¾ in to No. 4</td>
            <td>
              90–100% passing ¾ in; 20–55% passing ⅜ in; 0–10% passing No. 4
            </td>
            <td>
              Concrete mixes, tight utility trench bedding, clean architectural
              paths
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">#78</th>
            <td>½ in to No. 8</td>
            <td>
              90–100% passing ½ in; 40–75% passing ⅜ in; 0–10% passing No. 8
            </td>
            <td>
              Surface chip seals, thin top-dressing, fine drainage overlays
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">#8</th>
            <td>⅜ in to No. 8</td>
            <td>
              85–100% passing ⅜ in; 10–30% passing No. 4; 0–10% passing No. 8
            </td>
            <td>
              Pea gravel / fine walkways, chip seal, tightly spaced paver joints
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">#89</th>
            <td>⅜ in to No. 16</td>
            <td>
              90–100% passing ⅜ in; 20–55% passing No. 4; 5–30% passing No. 8
            </td>
            <td>
              Masonry fills, thin ornamental beds, small aggregate concrete
              mixes
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Ask for the current grading sheet and the specification used to evaluate
      it. Compare the complete required sieve series, not only the largest stone
      visible on the pile.{" "}
      <a href="https://www.pa.gov/agencies/penndot/programs-and-doing-business/construction/aggregate-plant-book">
        PennDOT’s producer records
      </a>{" "}
      list separate grading worksheets for #57, #67, other coarse aggregates and
      base products. Its{" "}
      <a href="https://www.ecamms.penndot.pa.gov/Public/Pages/Bulletins/BulletinSearch.aspx?BulletinTypeKey=1">
        Bulletin 14 search
      </a>{" "}
      is a source for Pennsylvania aggregate approvals.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="57-stone" aria-labelledby="stone57-heading">
    <h2 id="stone57-heading">Understanding #57 stone before ordering</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>#57</strong> identifies a coarse aggregate grading in{" "}
      <a href="https://www.pa.gov/content/dam/copapwp-pagov/en/penndot/documents/public/pubsforms/publications/pub_408/408_2026/408_2026_ie.pdf">
        state highway
      </a>{" "}
      and ASTM D448 specifications. It is not a 57-millimeter particle diameter,
      a single mineral type, or a certification that every quarry load has been
      washed free of dust. One quarry sells crushed #57 limestone or granite;
      another sells rounded river stone meeting #57 sieve limits.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Source and shape:</strong> confirm mineral type (limestone,
        granite, trap rock, or rounded river stone).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fines and washing:</strong> request the No. 200 wash result when
        ordering for French drains or permeable bedding.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Loose bulk weight:</strong> ask the supplier for pounds or short
        tons per cubic yard (many #57 clean stone quotes fall near 1.30–1.45
        short tons/yd³).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A 100 sq ft layer of #57 stone at 3 inches deep occupies 0.93 yd³ before
      extra; with a 10% allowance, order 1.02 yd³ (about 1.38 short tons at 1.35
      tons/yd³). Estimate trench and pipe deductions on the{" "}
      <a href="